Experimental AME/Ztt support
===========================

The Ztt implementation in this directory is based on the draft AME/Ztt ISA
v0.6, specification commit 8d27acdcee7a189aad4e2907412e0c40a9d4b9e2.
The typed interface follows intrinsic v0.2.4 with the v0.2.5 zip/unzip value
interface and the separately agreed nominal Scalar design. This is a local,
experimental implementation, not a ratified ISA, stable API or psABI.

This source note applies to the Ztt definitions, builtins, header, RTL
patterns and corresponding AME tests. Repeated provenance comments are not
required at individual helpers or test cases. Keep comments that explain
state preservation, operand formation, allocation constraints or local
deviations; version provenance does not replace those explanations.

Instruction encodings remain provisional. The local writable CSR mapping
uses amestype=0x810, amefflags=0x811 and amexsat=0x812. These are not official
allocations and require matching Binutils and execution-platform support.
The architecture attribute alone does not identify this local mapping.

N is runtime-sized; UDS and M/ACC resources are selected by the compile-time
profile. Types and declarations do not imply that every hardware tuple is
supported. Typed values are function-local and require the documented
state and ownership restrictions.  Ordinary scalar/pointer calls can
clobber the M/ACC bank; live typed values are saved and restored, including
ACC descriptors, and typed operations prepare their required datatype.
Callees must return with compatible N/profile and enabled/owned state.
Typed arguments/returns (including varargs) and a stable typed calling
convention are not provided.  Explicit ownership changes in typed functions
require a successful acquire-bit or ameown guard proven over the CFG.
Query-only observers retain the caller-owned entry contract.  A proved
query guard permits unknown entry ownership and keeps typed state setup
on the owned path; merely observing ameown does not imply such a guard.
After an exceptional edge, every typed function must prove ownership again
before typed accesses, even if it contains no ownership query or transition.
This can be guarded inside catch or after the join.  A handler that returns
or rethrows without further typed access needs no new ownership proof.
Release invalidates prior status proofs; late checking includes generated
register accesses and reloads.  N/profile must remain stable for the entire
function, including unowned intervals, and MS must already be enabled.
Unknown paths and raw selector mixing remain rejected.  This is not a
guarantee for exceptional exits or arbitrary ownership-changing inline asm.

The user interface is documented in gcc/doc/extend.texi. Compiler regression
tests live in gcc.target/riscv/ame and g++.target/riscv/ame under testsuite,
with independent ame.exp drivers. SAIL/Spike joint execution programs and
raw build/test archives are maintained outside the GCC source tree.

Definition catalogs
-------------------

The catalogs are grouped by their consumer and ordering contract, rather
than by the historical implementation batches:

  riscv-ztt-builtins.def          Raw instruction selector builtins.
  riscv-ztt-profile.def           Compile-time resource profiles.
  riscv-ztt-types.def             Ordered M/ACC type families.
  riscv-ztt-operations.def        Reusable operation lists.
  riscv-ztt-intrinsics.def        Ordered typed catalog and family includes.
  riscv-ztt-wide-intrinsics.def   Wide integer families.
  riscv-ztt-int-intrinsics.def    Small integer families and matmul.
  riscv-ztt-fp-intrinsics.def     Floating-point families and matmul.
  riscv-ztt-exponent-scalar.def   Exponent scalar interfaces.
  riscv-ztt-memory128.def         Storage-only 128-bit memory interfaces.
  riscv-ztt-zip-value.def         Paired-value zip/unzip interfaces.

The type and operation catalogs select sections with the corresponding
entry macros.  Consumers define only the sections they need, include the
catalog, and undefine those entry macros afterwards.  Define all type
entry macros for the ordered type enum and metadata table.  These files
intentionally have no include guard; the shared iterator helpers have one.

Catalog order determines builtin/type identities used by registration and
LTO.  Keep family and row order stable when reorganizing files.  In
particular, the exponent, memory128 and zip families retain separate late
registration positions.  Consolidation must compare complete expanded
records, not just names or counts.  It does not reduce the number of
interfaces or change the lazy-registration rules.  Update t-riscv whenever
the include graph changes so incremental builds track every catalog.

Registration reuses a GC-rooted leaf/nothrow input list.  Each declaration
receives its own attribute nodes; option pragmas and declaration-specific
attributes must not modify the shared inputs.

The exponent Scalar catalog uses ZTT_EXPONENT_FAMILY to store family
descriptors and a shared type axis.  Expansion remains operation-major,
type-minor to preserve builtin codes and LTO identities.  Names are built
after profile/type checks.

Structural folding
------------------

The GIMPLE hook can replace extraction from a single-use concatenation
with the corresponding complete source value.  Exact types and shape
transitions must match.  The search stays within one basic block and
crosses only a bounded number of memory-free SSA copies or debug statements.
Calls, asm and state operations remain boundaries.  The concatenation
call is retained for ownership checking.  Multi-use pairs keep their
existing lowering to avoid extending the source halves' live ranges.

One same-type M-to-M copy may occur between concatenation and extraction.
Both intermediate values must have one use and consist of complete register
groups.  The copy also remains as an ownership witness.

A concatenation of complementary halves from the same parent can also
reuse that parent.  Both halves must have one use and the parent's only
value uses must be the two extractions.  The same local boundaries apply;
extraction calls remain as ownership witnesses.  Reversed indices and extra
live values keep their existing lowering.

Each half may pass through one same-type M-to-M copy.  Both the copy result
and extracted half must have one use, and all calls stay in the same local
window.  Copies retain their operands for ownership checking; longer chains
and fractional-register halves do not match.

Same-axis zip/unzip pairs can reuse their input in either order.  They
require the same complete 1x2 type, one value use of the input and
intermediate, and the same local boundaries.  The inner call remains for
ownership checking.  Different axes, repeated permutations and packed
types do not match.

One same-type M-to-M copy may occur between the inverse operations.  Both
intermediate values must have one use, and the copy joins the same bounded
window.  Inner layout and copy calls keep their operands for ownership
checking.  Copy chains and extra live intermediate values do not match.

Resultless typed extract and zip calls can remain as ownership witnesses
without counting as parent value uses.  Every use and witness must occur
in the same bounded local window.  The calls are retained; numeric, raw,
nonlocal and genuinely live uses do not gain this exception.

A resultless same-type M copy can also be a witness.  An extract or zip
result may have one use by such a copy; both calls join the bounded window
and retain their arguments.  The check does not follow further copies.

Zero-offset integer and floating row/column shifts of basic Squares expand
to complete-mode copies on optimized runtime profiles.  Floating values
retain their bits, including zero signs and NaN payloads.  The move stays
visible to early ownership checking even for an unused result.  Nonzero and
dynamic offsets, row/column broadcasts and O0 retain their existing lowering.

Local datatype preparation
--------------------------

ACC matmul shares source preparation when both operands are the same
allocated M group, mode and Md.  Output and instruction length use the same
predicate; before allocation, length assumes both sources need preparation.
Private MEM and scratch operands remain in RTL.

msettyp/asettyp clear data, so equal descriptors alone do not justify
removing a clear.  Shared-source reuse is confined to one composite and
retains ACC updates and arithmetic side effects.

ztt_md_reuse runs after allocation and scheduling, before variable tracking.
It tracks complete M groups and descriptor values within each block.
Matching matmul and single-ACC M-to-ACC sources skip save/set/reload;
internal operands control output and instruction length.  M-to-ACC still
sets Ad and moves the source.  Multiple-ACC and packed constructions keep
full preparation.

Calls, asm, unknown typed operations and unknown UNSPECs clear all facts.
Register definitions invalidate dependent descriptors and overlapping groups.
Full Pmode GPR single SETs establish constants through RTL simplification.
Prepared groups retain constant snapshots after the original GPR changes;
unknown descriptors require unchanged GPRs.  Partial/parallel definitions
invalidate constants, and memory contents are not tracked.
Typed loads can use an earlier-defined ordinary GPR holding the same full
descriptor already consumed by a typed load.  Fixed/global registers and
instruction clobbers are excluded;
dead materialization and register notes are updated after substitution.
Typed integer and canonical FP16/BF16/FP32/FP64 zero construction reuses
the bit clear performed by msettyp for a known descriptor and a complete
group.  Other descriptors and raw zeros retain their instructions.
Setters and arithmetic effects remain.
Typed zero broadcasts between known standard integer descriptors also reuse
the complete-group setter.  The whole scalar carrier must be zero; its dead
setup is removed and register notes are refreshed.  Argument effects remain.
Canonical floating broadcasts use the same rule when source and destination
formats match; their rounding modes may differ.  Cross-format broadcasts,
negative zero and unknown carriers retain their instructions.
Integer and canonical floating ACC zero construction reuses asettyp for
every member; the complete ACC mode and Ad writes remain.
It is disabled at O0.  Prepared single-ACC moves and matmul with both
sources prepared use patterns without private MEM, stride or scratch
operands.  Prepared matmul retains the tied old ACC and arithmetic effects,
but no longer needs source descriptor GPRs.  Fast RTL DCE removes their dead
address calculations before debug locations are computed.  Frame layout
and runtime-N validation remain unchanged.  Partly prepared matmul keeps
its private operands.
After DCE, leaf functions with no live stack references can
drop an unused AME workspace allocation/release pair.  Both adjustments
use the proven frame-size coefficient.  Cross-block pairs require matching
allocation state at joins and balanced normal exits.  Fixed
frames, N checks and CFA rules remain; calls, asm, real stack storage,
probing, alloca and RVV save areas exclude this cleanup.
For mixed matmul, a prepared side's unused descriptor is replaced with zero
after allocation.  The mask prevents its use; the other side retains its
descriptor and full save/set/reload sequence.  DCE can then remove the dead
descriptor computation without adding another family of RTL patterns.
Typed row-major, column-major and strided stores establish the source
group's Md for subsequent consumers.  When the complete source group is
already prepared, the store drops its own save/set/reload and private
operands.  The prepared pattern retains the destination MEM, user stride,
store kind and volatile state operation; the store itself stays in place.
ACC-to-M moves establish Md separately for each transfer packet.  Track
those subgroups independently, including packed ACC packets, rather than
treating a multiple-packet destination as one larger prepared group.
Grouped M-to-ACC moves also establish source Md per transfer packet.
Discard old source metadata and explicit scratch clobbers before recording
these subgroups, retaining facts for disjoint M registers.
Same-datatype binary operations establish Md for both complete source
groups and the result.  Record all three after explicit clobbers so later
stores and ACC consumers can reuse their preparation.
Binary operations also reuse matching incoming source Md independently.
Matrix and scalar elementwise operations establish each M packet's Md
separately, with destination facts taking precedence on register reuse.
Scalar operands and amestype are not M datatype state.
Old-destination matrix operations establish Md in destination/source order.
Scalar old-destination operations record only D and the M source, not TC
or the fixed exponent. Compatible overlapping groups keep the last setup.
Whole-register loads and stores retain known physical Md without inferring
datatype from the transferred payload.
When both sources match, a prepared form drops the private memory and
address operands while retaining any required destination setup.
Identical physical binary inputs share their first complete preparation,
including when incoming Md is unknown.
Typed broadcast and index constructors establish their complete result
group's Md for later consumers in the same block.
Conversions and structural unary operations establish source and result Md
per datatype packet.  Do not combine adjacent packets into a larger fact.
Row/column broadcasts and shifts establish the same Md for the complete
source and result basic Squares, including an in-place result.
